---
import Component from "../../layouts/Component.astro"
import Anatomy from "../../components/Anatomy.astro"
import AutoExample from "../../components/AutoExample.astro"
import Conditional from "../../components/Conditional.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import KeyboardSupport from "./partials/KeyboardSupport.astro"
import Usage from "./partials/Usage.astro"
import PropOrClass from "../../components/PropOrClass.astro"
import { Rating as UIRating } from "opui-css/astro"

import RatingCSS from "@opui/css/components/rating.css?raw"

import ratingApi from "../../component-api/rating/api"

export const description =
  "A star rating: a meter that shows a score, or radios that collect one."
---

<Component
  description={description}
  slug="rating"
  heroAnatomy
  apis={[{ title: "Rating API", api: ratingApi }]}
  browserSupport={["attr", "has", "relative-color", "user-pseudos"]}
  changelogPaths={[
    { path: "packages/opui/components/Rating", type: "component" },
    { path: "src/docs/components/rating.astro", type: "docs" },
    { path: "src/component-examples/rating", type: "docs" },
    { path: "packages/opui/css/components/rating.css", type: "css" },
    { path: "src/component-api/rating", type: "api" },
  ]}
  installationTabs={[{ title: "rating.css", code: RatingCSS, lang: "css" }]}
>
  <Fragment slot="title">Rating</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    A star rating. A <code>&lt;meter&gt;</code> shows a score, and a group of radios
    collects one. For a plain choice between options, use a <DocLink
      href="/components/radio">Radio</DocLink
    >, and for a value on a scale, a <DocLink href="/components/range"
      >Range</DocLink
    >.
  </Fragment>

  <Usage slot="usage">
    <li slot="when">
      A score next to a product or a review, like 4.7 out of 5, as a read-only <a
        href="#basics">meter</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li slot="when">
      A review form that asks for a score, with the <a href="#input">input</a>.
    </li>
    <li slot="when-not">
      Progress toward a goal, like a download. Use a <DocLink
        href="/components/progress">Progress</DocLink
      > bar.
    </li>
    <li slot="when-not">
      A number on a continuous scale, like a volume. Use a <DocLink
        href="/components/range">Range</DocLink
      >.
    </li>
  </Usage>

  <Fragment slot="anatomy">
    <Anatomy>
      <UIRating label="Your rating" name="anatomy-rating" value={3} />
    </Anatomy>
  </Fragment>

  <section id="basics">
    <h2>Basics</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >Add <code>.ui-rating</code> to a <code>&lt;meter&gt;</code> with <code
          >max</code
        > and <code>value</code>. The <code>aria-label</code> is its accessible name,
        so put the score in it.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="components"
        >Without <code>name</code>, a rating is a read-only <code
          >&lt;meter&gt;</code
        >. The <code>label</code> is its accessible name, so put the score in it.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>
    <AutoExample name="Basics" />
  </section>

  <section id="input">
    <h2>Input</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >Add <code>.ui-rating</code> to a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> with a <code
          >&lt;legend&gt;</code
        > and one radio per star, named with <code>aria-label</code>. The radio
        with <code>value="0"</code> clears the rating.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="react"
        >Set <code>name</code> to collect a rating. It renders a <code
          >&lt;fieldset&gt;</code
        > of radios, with the <code>label</code> as its legend. Follow the rating
        with <code>value</code> and <code>onChange</code>, or set <code
          >defaultValue</code
        >.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="svelte"
        >Set <code>name</code> to collect a rating. It renders a <code
          >&lt;fieldset&gt;</code
        > of radios, with the <code>label</code> as its legend. Bind the rating with
        <code>bind:value</code>.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="vue"
        >Set <code>name</code> to collect a rating. It renders a <code
          >&lt;fieldset&gt;</code
        > of radios, with the <code>label</code> as its legend. Bind the rating with
        <code>v-model</code>.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="components"
        >Set <code>name</code> to collect a rating. It renders a <code
          >&lt;fieldset&gt;</code
        > of radios, with the <code>label</code> as its legend.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>
    <p>
      Hovering previews a rating, and a click or the arrow keys set it. The icon
      before the stars clears the rating, and stays visible so touch users can
      find it. The count after the stars is a CSS counter.
    </p>
    <AutoExample name="Input" />
  </section>

  <section id="sizes">
    <h2>Sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Resize a rating with <PropOrClass prop='size="small"' class=".ui-small" /> or
      <PropOrClass prop='size="large"' class=".ui-large" />. Input stars are
      bigger than meter stars, so every radio is a large enough target.
    </p>
    <AutoExample name="Sizes" row />
  </section>

  <section id="fractions">
    <h2>Fractions</h2>
    <p>
      The meter fills the exact fraction, so 4.9 doesn't round up to five stars.
    </p>
    <AutoExample name="Fractions" column />
  </section>

  <section id="max">
    <h2>Max</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="html"
        ><code>max</code> sets the number of stars, read with typed <code
          >attr()</code
        >. Browsers without it read <code>--_max</code>, so set both to the same
        number, or they draw five stars.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="components"
        ><code>max</code> sets the number of stars. Defaults to <code>5</code
        >.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>
    <AutoExample name="Max" column />
  </section>

  <section id="disabled">
    <h2>Disabled</h2>
    <p>Set <code>disabled</code> to disable every radio.</p>
    <AutoExample name="Disabled" />
  </section>

  <section id="validation">
    <h2>Validation</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >Set <code>required</code> on the radios and leave out the clear radio, so
        a star has to be picked. <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> on the radios tints
        the empty stars. Point <code>aria-describedby</code> at the error message.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="components"
        ><code>required</code> drops the clear radio, so a star has to be picked.
        <code>error</code> marks the radios invalid and tints the empty stars. Point
        <code>aria-describedby</code> at the error message.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>
    <AutoExample name="Validation" />
  </section>

  <section id="localization">
    <h2>Localization</h2>
    <p>
      In right-to-left text the meter fills from the right, and the radios and
      arrow keys follow the reading direction.
    </p>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >Translate the <code>aria-label</code> of each radio.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="components"
        >The radios are named "No rating", "1 star", "2 stars" and so on.
        Translate them with <code>clearLabel</code> and <code>starLabel</code>,
        a function that gets the star's value. Use <code>Intl.PluralRules</code> for
        languages with more plural forms.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>
    <AutoExample name="Localization" />
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="accessibility">
    <KeyboardSupport />
    <p>
      The meter is announced with its label and value. Screen readers may read
      the value as a percentage, so put the score in the label, such as "Rated
      4.5 out of 5".
    </p>
    <p>
      The input is a group of native radios named by the legend. Each radio has
      its own name, such as "3 stars", and the arrow keys move between them. The
      count after the stars is hidden from screen readers, since the checked
      radio already says it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Radios get the library's focus ring, and every one is at least as wide as
      <code>--ui-control-size-x-small</code>. In forced colors mode filled stars
      use <code>SelectedItem</code> on the input and <code>CanvasText</code> on the
      meter, and empty stars use <code>GrayText</code>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="under-the-hood">
    <p>
      The meter and the radios draw their stars with one SVG <code
        >--_star-image</code
      > mask. <code>meter.ui-rating</code> is <code
        >attr(max type(&lt;number&gt;), var(--_max))</code
      > stars wide and masked with <code>repeat-x</code>, so the browser's own
      fill (<code>::-webkit-meter-optimum-value</code>, <code
        >::-moz-meter-bar</code
      >) shows through as filled stars, down to a fraction of one. The empty
      stars read <code>--ui-track-bg-color</code> when a theme sets it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In <code>fieldset.ui-rating</code> each radio is <code
        >appearance: none</code
      >
      with the star in <code>input::before</code>. <code
        >input:is(:checked, :has(~ :checked))</code
      > fills every star up to the checked one, and under <code
        >(hover: hover)</code
      >
      <code>input:is(:hover, :has(~ input:hover))</code> previews a rating instead.
      <code>input[value="0"]</code> is the clear icon.
    </p>
    <p>
      The count after the stars is <code>fieldset::after</code> printing two counters,
      <code>ui-rating</code>, incremented by the filled stars, and <code
        >ui-rating-max</code
      >, by every star but the clear radio, with empty alternative text so it
      isn't read.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</Component>
